  "articles": [
    {
      "sequence_id": 1,
      "text": "Spanish nouns are classified as either masculine or feminine, an inherent property of each noun that does not change. Articles and adjectives must agree with the gender of the noun they modify. Definite articles are ‘el’ for masculine and ‘la’ for feminine, while indefinite articles are ‘un’ for masculine and ‘una’ for feminine. Many adjectives have distinct masculine and feminine endings: the masculine form often ends in -o and the feminine in -a (e.g., ‘mucho/mucha’). Other adjectives, particularly those ending in -e or a consonant (like ‘mineral’, ‘tras’, ‘siete’), do not change for gender. When learning a noun, it is useful to memorize it with its definite article to remember its gender."
    }
  ],
